  "summary": "Recent preprints pursue nonreciprocal and strongly confined optical responses via 2D semiconductors, Floquet metasurfaces, magnonic cavities, and ENZ oxides.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is control of optical response beyond static, reciprocal, linear behavior in integrated and sheet-based platforms. A computational study proposes integrated optical isolators built from nonreciprocal 2D semiconductors that exploit spin-momentum locking, including an all-optical isolator concept. A separate study uses temporally modulated Floquet metasurfaces built from three closely spaced conducting sheets to report asymmetric frequency conversion, which is presented as violating Kirchhoff's law of thermal radiation. An experimental van der Waals cavity magnonic device with hierarchical anti-crossing reports reduced magnon loss and a tunable coupled-mode Hamiltonian. A theoretical inverse-design study of transparent conducting oxide cavities at the epsilon-near-zero point reports stronger light confinement and faster dynamics. What is new across these items is the engineering of nonreciprocity, modulation, and coupling in compact geometries, though the items differ in method and maturity and do not share a single mechanism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
